runit is a small init and service supervisor, with real manuals (smarden.org/runit).
runit-init runs three stages: /etc/runit/1 (setup, then the /etc/rc.d/boot/*.sh scripts
from base-bootscripts, so udev, fsck and the fstab mounts run), /etc/runit/2 (runsvdir
supervises /etc/service) and /etc/runit/3 (shutdown). Service definitions live in
/etc/runit/sv/<name>/run and are enabled with a symlink in /etc/service. Ships the net
(dhcp) and getty-tty1 services, and poweroff/reboot/halt wrappers that use runit-init
under runit and fall back to situation's own commands under situation.
Boot it with init=/sbin/runit-init on the kernel command line.
Day to day: sv status /etc/service/*, sv restart net, ln -s /etc/runit/sv/x /etc/service/x
